## 1. vm.h - Update the VM Structure

I replaced the fixed-size stack and stackTop pointer with a dynamically allocated array and two integer fields.

```c
typedef struct {
  Chunk* chunk;
  uint8_t* ip;

  Value* stack;
  int stackCount;
  int stackCapacity;
} VM;
```

- stack stores the dynamically allocated values.
- stackCount tracks how many values are currently on the stack.
- stackCapacity tracks the total number of allocated slots.

The original STACK_MAX constant is no longer needed for this implementation.

---

## 2. vm.c - Initialize the Stack

I updated resetStack() and initVM() to initialize the dynamic stack.

```c
static void resetStack() {
  vm.stackCount = 0;
}

void initVM() {
  vm.stack = NULL;
  vm.stackCapacity = 0;
  resetStack();
}
```

The stack starts empty and does not allocate memory until a value is pushed.

---

## 3. vm.c - Update push()

I modified push() so that it checks whether there is enough available space before storing a value.

```c
void push(Value value) {
  if (vm.stackCapacity < vm.stackCount + 1) {
    int oldCapacity = vm.stackCapacity;

    vm.stackCapacity = GROW_CAPACITY(oldCapacity);

    vm.stack = GROW_ARRAY(
        Value,
        vm.stack,
        oldCapacity,
        vm.stackCapacity);
  }

  vm.stack[vm.stackCount] = value;
  vm.stackCount++;
}
```

When the stack is full, GROW_CAPACITY() increases its capacity, and GROW_ARRAY() reallocates the memory.

This uses the memory management helpers already defined in Crafting Interpreters.

---

## 4. vm.c - Update pop()

I changed pop() to use stackCount instead of stackTop.

```c
Value pop() {
  vm.stackCount--;
  return vm.stack[vm.stackCount];
}
```

The function decreases stackCount and returns the most recently pushed value.

---

## 5. vm.c - Free the Stack

Since the stack now allocates memory dynamically, the VM must release that memory when it is no longer needed.

```c
void freeVM() {
  FREE_ARRAY(Value, vm.stack, vm.stackCapacity);

  vm.stack = NULL;
  vm.stackCount = 0;
  vm.stackCapacity = 0;
}
```

This prevents allocated stack memory from being left behind when the interpreter shuts down.

---

## 6. vm.c - Update Stack Tracing

The original debugging code uses the stackTop pointer to display the stack contents.

I updated the tracing code to use stackCount.

```c
printf("          ");

for (int i = 0; i < vm.stackCount; i++) {
  printf("[ ");
  printValue(vm.stack[i]);
  printf(" ]");
}

printf("\n");
```

This displays all the active values currently stored in the stack.

